{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh's direction, 2026-10-11 17:45 Eastern: the demo's words follow the platform's own split, and the plain words are shared across the three applications on purpose.\n\nThe problem. The model needs about twenty-five exact terms (scope, entry, act, definition, lane, extent, rules scope, destination, register, directory, commitment, hold, plan, concern, report, input, manifest, job, result, link, grant, guard, effect, reservation, receipt). The demo does not. Plan 019 already says the demo uses the GitHub user's words, and the model's words appear only where a rule or a participant uses one. This request makes that a rule with a glossary, and adds the constraint the platform view imposes.\n\nThe rule, in three parts, applied to every caption, page label and narrated line:\n1. If the word names a thing the application owns, use the application's word. The repository says issue, pull request, assign, merge, branch, clone. The Counter says turn, count, next. The jam says theme, instrument, solo, bar.\n2. If the word names something the room does to that thing, use the one shared plain word, the same in all three segments. These are the platform's words on the surface: a promise (commitment: offered, taken, kept), working on it with a time limit (hold), allowed to (grant), refused because (a failed guard, with the plain reason, never the guard's name), the rules, the record, the room.\n3. If the word names a rule, say which rule.\n\nWhy the sharing matters. When the video says \"the same fabric\" and cuts from the repository to the Counter to the band, the viewer can only see sameness if the same few plain words recur: \"Paul promised issue #1\", \"the synth agent promised the next bar\", \"device B promised the next count\". If the repository segment says assigned, the Counter says claimed and the jam says took the synth, the sameness is asserted, not seen.\n\nThe glossary (record word, then the surface word):\nscope, entry, act: the room, a step, an action.\ncommitment (offered, accepted, fulfilled): a promise, offered, taken, kept.\nhold: working on it, with a time limit.\nplan, concern: the split, a part.\nreport, accept-report: handed in, accepted.\nmanifest: a version of the change.\nintegration commit: the combined commit.\nextent: an area of the repository.\nrules scope, controller: the rules, the person who owns them.\ndestination: the repository (the model keeps \"destination\": it is the role a repository plays for a room, and a Counter or jam room has one too).\nregister, directory, membership, inbox: not said.\ngrant: allowed to.\nguard failed with reason x: refused, because x, in plain words.\nreservation, receipt, first head: not said; \"published\" and \"checked\".\n\nFour words stay as they are because they carry the claim: room, record, rules, refused.\n\nSee plans/019-2026-10-05-demo-story-direction.md, plans/025-2026-10-06-current-manual-inventory.md (nouns, verbs, invariants), the demo plan re-cut (plan 030) and notes/2026-10-07-demo-script-draft.md or its successor.","body":{"conditions":"The demo plan (plan 030 or its successor) and the shot list carry the words rule and the glossary below; every caption, page label and narrated line in the shot list passes the three-part test; the same plain word is used for each platform term in the repository, Counter and jam segments; a short glossary section is added to the demo script document; the planner reports the commit in the sprint-cadence thread and the next sprint report.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}